WEBVTT

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:09.390
We have an innate curiosity about our origins.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.390
Is life in our galaxy common?

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:14.670
Does it exist?

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:15.670
Are we alone?

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.890
How unique is life here on Earth?

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.980
The Kepler mission is one step in answering that question.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:45.580
The Kepler mission is a huge step forward in answering the question, what is our place

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:46.340
in the universe?

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:53.180
The Kepler mission is NASA's first mission capable of finding Earth-like planets orbiting

00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:59.140
other stars in the galaxy in a region around the star we call the habitable zone.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.640
The habitable zone is where we think water will be.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:04.520
If you can find liquid water on the surface,

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.420
we think we may very well find life there.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.580
So that zone is not too close to the star

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.920
because it's too hot and the water boils,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.380
not too far away where the water is condensed

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:15.520
and ice covered, planet covered with glaciers.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.070
It's the Goldilocks zone.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:20.280
Not too hot, not too cold, just right for life.

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:26.810
The primary science goal of Kepler is to answer the question

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.390
if Earth-like planets in our galaxy are common or rare.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.480
Kepler is going to detect planets via the transit method.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.020
What we aim to do is to measure the brightnesses of stars very, very accurately

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.580
for a very long period of time without blinking,

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.900
so that when the planet passes in front of the star,

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.690
the light output from the star is going to dim just a little bit.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:51.640
That star is going to blink for about 12 hours,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.250
and we will be able to measure that dip in brightness.

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.920
And it's that dip that we call a transit.

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.980
Kepler is a different animal from the Hubble.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.240
Hubble Space Telescope or the Spitzer Infrared Telescope because it doesn't take pictures.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.330
You won't see beautiful images that you see from Hubble because we're not doing that kind

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:10.810
of science.

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:16.630
Those types of missions like Hubble and Spitzer are designed to focus on specific targets.

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:23.960
If you hold your hand at arm's length with a grain of salt or a grain of sand, that's

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:29.360
about how much of the sky you can see with a normal astronomical telescope at any one

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:29.660
time.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:33.790
Kepler has a field of view that's 10 degrees by 10 degrees.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:40.340
Our field of view is about as big as your hand at arm's length covering the sky.

00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:44.300
So rather than taking pictures of very concentrated parts of the sky,

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:47.770
we're looking at taking a very broad snapshot of a lot of stars.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.230
Kepler takes really fuzzy pictures.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:55.910
But the thing that Kepler is designed to do, and it does very well,

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.860
is look for very small variations in the brightness.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.720
of the stars. An Earth-like planet passing in front of a sun-like star is going to cause the

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:12.740
brightness of that star to dim by only one part per 10,000. That's like looking at a headlight

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:18.590
from a great distance and trying to sense the brightness change when a flea crawls across the

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:24.510
surface. But the Kepler instrument is designed to detect such small changes in brightness.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.980
Kepler sensors are CCDs. CCD in your...

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.750
Your camera is about the size of one of your small fingernails.

00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:41.780
The CCD chips on Kepler are a couple of inches square in pairs, and there's 42 of those.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.900
In your camera, you might have a few megapixels.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:49.080
If you have a really nice camera, you might have 10 megapixels.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:56.050
We have 96 million pixels, and they have very low noise, so we can find very small planets.

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.910
Once Kepler is launched from the

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.560
it will go into what's called an earth trailing orbit.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.790
Astronomers call that a heliocentric orbit,

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:07.170
which means sun-centered orbit.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.720
It looks at the star's continuously, day and night,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.680
365 days a year, and it slowly moves away from the earth,

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.180
orbiting the sun by itself.

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:18.910
And from there it will point itself at the Kepler field

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:23.000
and stare at the Kepler field nearly without blinking

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.880
for three and a half years at least,

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:26.960
maybe longer if we have an extended mission.

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:29.960
Kepler will be looking in a...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:34.290
region of the sky that you'll see almost every night in the summertime.

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:39.490
The field of view is kind of nestled underneath the wing of Cygnus, the swan, between Cygnus

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:39.930
and Lyra.

00:04:41.220 --> 00:04:46.240
Now in that area of sky, there are something like six and a half million stars.

00:04:46.910 --> 00:04:48.030
Why do we need so many?

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:53.210
Because of the way Kepler looks for planets, we have to have planetary systems that are

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:58.240
lined up so that the planet actually passes between the star and the Kepler telescope

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:58.980
in its orbit.

00:04:59.260 --> 00:04:59.960
Probability.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.160
of that happening if you just throw stars randomly down in our galaxy is something like

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:08.820
one to ten percent. It's small. So of those six and a half million scattered over that

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:16.500
100 square degrees, only maybe 200,000 are interesting to us. We select the 170,000 or

00:05:16.500 --> 00:05:22.660
so that are most suitable to look at for planets with the Kepler telescope. Finally,

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:28.560
we expect to end up with somewhere between a few hundred and a few thousand signals

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:29.880
that are really planets.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.400
around the stars that we're looking at.

00:05:33.710 --> 00:05:36.240
Well, this is the first time that photometry,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.100
the measurement of the brightness of stars,

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.580
will ever be done at this level of precision.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:45.590
And it will be done simultaneously on more than 100,000 stars

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:47.590
continuously over a period of years.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.730
We're somewhat like pioneers, very much like the explorers

00:05:53.730 --> 00:05:55.950
that we're looking for new lands to settle.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:58.080
And that's how I see what we're doing.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:05:59.920
We're looking in this region of our galaxy.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:01.950
for other habitable worlds.

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.980
I think people everywhere want to know

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.980
whether with all the stars out there

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:09.080
do they have planets that are Earth-sized?

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.690
Are Earths frequent or are they rare?

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.260
And this gives us that answer.

00:06:15.390 --> 00:06:18.660
It's the next step, mankind's exploration of the galaxy.

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:22.400
There's two extreme answers that Kepler might give us.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.300
It might tell us that there's an Earth-like planet

00:06:25.300 --> 00:06:27.660
practically around every star we look at.

00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:29.250
Imagine!

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.380
possibilities for life in the galaxy or life in the universe.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:38.780
If, on the other hand, we found out that Earth was very special and that the typical star

00:06:38.780 --> 00:06:44.320
like the Sun is not hosting planets like the Earth, I think that would be a real surprise

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.440
as well and would really make us reconsider the special nature of Earth.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:55.420
We are now finally at the point where our technology allows us to build a machine that

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:59.090
can make the observations to scientifically answer this question.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.800
How many stars like the Sun did develop planets around them like the Earth?

00:07:04.590 --> 00:07:07.810
I think that's such an important question to everyone and so interesting that we really

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:10.470
have to do it and it's a once in a lifetime opportunity right now.
